WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.720
How many devices hold our company data,
and how many could we wipe tonight?

00:00:05.060 --> 00:00:07.010
Four ways a device fails us.

00:00:08.350 --> 00:00:10.700
First, a laptop is left in a taxi.

00:00:11.095 --> 00:00:14.165
A login password protects the screen,
not the disk.

00:00:14.515 --> 00:00:15.325
Without encryption,

00:00:15.735 --> 00:00:17.705
every file is readable in minutes.

00:00:18.065 --> 00:00:23.355
That is usually a reportable GDPR breach,
with a 72-hour clock.

00:00:23.615 --> 00:00:27.225
With encryption on,
it is a hardware replacement and a form.

00:00:28.250 --> 00:00:31.320
Second, company mail on a
phone we have never seen.

00:00:31.615 --> 00:00:34.365
We cannot enforce a screen lock,
check encryption,

00:00:34.735 --> 00:00:36.385
or wipe it when it is lost.

00:00:36.760 --> 00:00:39.350
The downloaded files outlive the employee.

00:00:39.725 --> 00:00:42.335
The point is not banning personal devices.

00:00:42.820 --> 00:00:46.070
Devices we can wipe,
update and lock are welcome.

00:00:46.380 --> 00:00:48.110
The rest read mail in the browser.

00:00:49.350 --> 00:00:51.840
Third, everyone is
their own administrator.

00:00:52.235 --> 00:00:56.145
One click on a fake PDF tool
installs it system-wide.

00:00:56.435 --> 00:00:58.885
It switches off the
security tools and hides.

00:00:59.255 --> 00:01:02.825
Removing admin rights costs a
few weeks of helpdesk friction.

00:01:03.225 --> 00:01:05.435
Keeping them costs the first incident.

00:01:06.483 --> 00:01:09.313
Fourth, the browser is
the new operating system.

00:01:09.578 --> 00:01:10.808
Updates wait weeks,

00:01:11.178 --> 00:01:13.188
a fake update banner delivers malware,

00:01:13.758 --> 00:01:16.428
and a resold extension reads every page.

00:01:16.783 --> 00:01:20.423
All of it happens inside the
logged-in session, after MFA,

00:01:20.423 --> 00:01:22.773
and needs no admin rights.

00:01:23.883 --> 00:01:25.093
What good looks like.

00:01:25.378 --> 00:01:27.508
Disk encryption and remote wipe.

00:01:27.833 --> 00:01:31.623
No standing local admin,
and an approved software catalogue.

00:01:31.998 --> 00:01:35.248
Auto-update within days,
and an extension allow-list.

00:01:35.563 --> 00:01:39.293
These are settings in the device
management we already pay for.

00:01:39.558 --> 00:01:41.248
NIS2 names cyber hygiene,

00:01:41.998 --> 00:01:44.388
encryption and access
control as risk measures.

00:01:46.263 --> 00:01:47.473
A suggested first step.

00:01:47.758 --> 00:01:49.028
Ask two questions.

00:01:49.333 --> 00:01:51.663
How many devices hold company data?

00:01:52.013 --> 00:01:53.623
And how many could we wipe tonight?

00:01:53.988 --> 00:01:57.238
If the second number is smaller,
that gap is the risk.

00:01:58.683 --> 00:02:00.333
Four ways a device fails.

00:02:00.633 --> 00:02:02.683
Count the devices we cannot wipe.

